WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:07.340
David, there's a lot of discussion about income
inequality if we accept that it's a problem.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:08.800
How can we actually fix it?

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.030
OK, this is a good question.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.440
Let's not debate whether income inequality
is a problem.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:15.840
Let let's accept that it is.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.820
And that's not what this segment is about.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.970
We can talk about whether it's a problem in
another segment.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:20.970
I believe that it is.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:25.350
And I've outlined why many times the most
important.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:32.140
Fact to realize about fixing income inequality
is that we don't need to come up with anything

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:33.140
new.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:38.470
We have essentially all the ideas we need
to deal with income inequality.

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:43.880
Now, to be very clear, I don't believe that
we need perfect distribution of income, perfect

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:49.480
distribution, equality of outcomes, so to
speak, is not naturally occurring anywhere.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.460
I don't believe it would be good to force
it.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:52.730
I'm not a socialist.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:56.100
I don't believe there would be any reasonable,
reasonable expectation that everybody has

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.100
the same amount of money.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.870
What I do think is a social Democrat, though,
is the bottom should be lifted.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.830
There should be a safety net that says we're
not going to allow people to ball fall below

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.140
this level.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:11.840
And then at the top, you should have more
restrictions about, OK, if we say billionaires

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:18.760
are okay, are multibillionaires turbow billionaires,
what at what point do we want to be saying

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.170
now is when you've got to really be redistributing?

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:22.320
It's a gray area.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.840
Different people would put it at a different
different spot.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.080
We have all of the different tools.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.200
But the problem is getting the right combination.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.490
So as an example, should the minimum wage
be higher than it is right now?

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:34.490
Of course.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:38.860
Is a fifteen dollars minimum wage nationally
going to solve the problem of inequality?

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:40.880
No, we need a higher minimum wage.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.479
Exactly what it should be probably depends
on the state that we're in.

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:48.970
But a fifteen dollar minimum wage isn't even
a living wage in many states based on cost

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:49.970
of living.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:51.570
So do we need to raise the minimum wage?

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:56.090
Yes, there will be people who come in and
say, well, for higher minimum wages, better,

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:58.490
why not just make it 200 dollars an hour?

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.299
That's not an equilibrium wage anywhere.

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:02.440
And that's a red herring.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:03.689
Don't fall for it.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:08.760
Do we need a more robust social service system
funded by taxes on the very, very rich, but

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.180
that includes financial transactions and some
of these other ideas?

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.390
Yeah, we've got the tools.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:14.870
We know what it would look like.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:18.270
There's just no political will to do it right
now.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:23.319
Do we need better educational access regardless
of income?

00:02:23.319 --> 00:02:26.459
Because one of the great equalizers is education.

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:29.940
And unfortunately, in our society, yes, there
are scholarships.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.069
Yes, we know about all that stuff.

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:37.560
But the reality is there is very low income
mobility in the United States.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:43.280
And part of that is because there is not widespread
good education that is accessible to people

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.010
who start off in an economically disadvantaged
position.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.010
Trade is part of it.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:49.540
You know, Trump talks about trade.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:51.550
Some trade things can be bad for the middle
class.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:54.070
Look, I'll concede that you've got to look
at trade.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:57.780
We can look at things like universal basic
income or a value added tax.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.970
And there's pros and cons to all of these.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:06.640
My point is there are no new tools that are
necessary to deal with income inequality.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:12.090
But we have to get away from do nothing and
let the markets handle it because we know

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:13.540
that doesn't work.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:14.599
We know that doesn't work.

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:22.459
I'm fine as a social Democrat, letting markets
work to direct resources in many industries.

00:03:22.459 --> 00:03:27.200
As I've said before, I don't need the government
making all the cell phones or making all the

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.510
airplanes, but I sure as hell want the government
involved in regulating those industries and

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:36.920
well regulated markets where private industry
is manufacturing stuff.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:43.950
I'm fine with certain areas need to be taken
out of that health care education.

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:48.840
You could argue that some basic level of housing
should be taken out of that.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:53.360
It's a debate in the gray area, but we've
got all the tools we need to figure out how

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:55.959
to put them together in the right proportions.

